 the 2020 crash and 64% in the 2022 bear market."],"val":["The fair-value models disagree (dispersion 97%), so treat the estimate as noisy.","Multiples send mixed signals against Healthcare peers: cheaper on P/FCF 18.0 vs 23.8, richer on P/E 164.2 vs 26.2 (sector median).","Against its own history it is cheap: the EBIT multiple sits 23% below its 10-year median.","Earnings yields are mixed: an FCF yield of 5.5% (above the risk-free rate) and an EBIT/EV of 4.9% (vs the 8% bar).","The 5 covering analysts target $24.10 on average, 11% below the price."],"prof":["ROIC of 7% falls short of the cost of capital of 8.4%, so growth at these returns destroys value; returns have swung a lot from year to year (CV 0.50).","It converts 6% of revenue into free cash flow.","Earnings quality is a concern: cash conversion of only -3.52x.","It spends 0.4% of revenue on stock compensation."],"hlth":["Leverage is heavy at 5.9x net debt to EBITDA ($239.9M of cash against $3.6B of debt), and interest cover is thin at 1.5x.","Warning signs: an Altman Z of 0.83 in the distress zone.","Goodwill makes up 64% of assets, a soft asset exposed to impairment."],"growth":["Revenue has compounded at 9.2% over 3 years, 12.2% over 5 years and 13.2% over 10 years, so growth is decelerating, and it has been lumpy (year-to-year swings of 15%).","Net reinvestment is nil (capex and working capital no more than cover depreciation), so fundamental growth is near zero.","Free cash flow has compounded -0.8% a year over five years, short of the 5% durability bar.","Capex runs only 0.4x depreciation (2.4% of sales), so the asset base is being harvested rather than grown.","Its Rule of 40 score is 18, below the 40 that marks a healthy growth business.","It has missed earnings estimates by 7.5% on average."],"own":["Insiders own a modest 1.8%, and reported institutional holdings exceed 100% of shares (a sign of heavy share lending).","Insider trading is two-way: 1 purchase and 6 sales over the past year, net $556K